Here is a concise ERD review, what we changed conceptually, and how that maps to the code that was added.

ERD review — major issues
Purchasing
The diagram tied Student directly to OrderItem (“Purchase”). In a normalized checkout model, the buyer should own the order header: Student → Order (1:m) → OrderItem (1:m), with each line item pointing at Course and carrying a price snapshot. OrderItem should not need a Student foreign key if Order already has StudentId.

LectureProgress → Course
Lecture already belongs to Section → Course, so a direct LectureProgress → Course link duplicates that path and can get out of sync. Progress is fully defined by (Lecture, Student); Course is derived by joining through Lecture → Section → Course (or a view/query when you need it).

Course attribute
Discription is a spelling error; the model uses Description.

User security
Storing a raw Password on the entity is not appropriate; the model uses PasswordHash (how you hash is an app concern).

Student ↔ Category
A single “Has” line is ambiguous. It is modeled explicitly as a many-to-many with a join entity StudentCategory (e.g. interests or followed categories), mirroring Course–Category with CourseCategory.

Student ↔ Course (enrollment)
The M:N enrollment is explicit as Enrollment (student + course + EnrolledAt), instead of an implied link only.

Derived fields
InstructorProfile metrics (e.g. average rating, totals) and Order.TotalAmount are still represented: instructor fields are denormalized/cached values you refresh in app or jobs; TotalAmount is the usual stored total at checkout for the order.

