Quick relationship summary (fixed ERD)
User 1:0..1 Student and 1:0..1 InstructorProfile (by role you only materialize the relevant row).
Instructor 1:m Course; Course m:n Category via CourseCategory; Student m:n Category via StudentCategory; Student m:n Course via Enrollment.
Course 1:m Section 1:m Lecture; Lecture m:n Student via LectureProgress (no direct course on progress).
Student 1:m Order; Order 1:m OrderItem; Order 1:0..1 Payment.
Review m:1 Student and m:1 Course, with at most one review per student per course in the database.
If you want next steps, we can add a first migration (dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate) and wire IUnitOfWork in Program.cs, or switch the connection string to SQL Server instead of SQLite.

Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore is only the core: change tracking, LINQ, model building, etc. It does not talk to any real database by itself.

Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite is the SQLite provider: it plugs into EF Core and implements SQL generation, connections, migrations, and SQLite-specific types for UseSqlite(...).

So you need some provider package whenever you want a real database:

If you call…	You need…
UseSqlite(...)
Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite
UseSqlServer(...)
Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer
UseNpgsql(...)
Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL
We added SQLite so the API could run locally with a simple file (studyhub.db) and no separate DB server. If you move to SQL Server (or another engine), you’d swap the package and UseSqlite for that provider’s package and Use* method—not keep SQLite unless you still want SQLite.